    <h2>Average Solar Panel Costs by System Size</h2>
    <p>Most UK homes install somewhere between a 3kW and 6kW system. As a rough guide, based on current UK installer pricing:</p>
    <ul>
    <li><strong>3kW system:</strong> £5,000 – £6,500</li>
      <li><strong>4kW system:</strong> £6,500 – £8,500</li>
      <li><strong>5kW system:</strong> £7,000 – £9,500</li>
      <li><strong>6kW system:</strong> £8,000 – £11,000</li>



    </ul>
    <p>These figures typically include panels, inverter, mounting, and installation labour.</p>

    <h2>What Affects the Price?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Panel type:</strong> Monocrystalline panels cost more than older polycrystalline panels but perform better, especially in limited roof space.</li>
      <li><strong>Roof type and access:</strong> Scaffolding, roof pitch, and tile type can add to labour costs.</li>
      <li><strong>Battery storage:</strong> Adding a home battery typically adds £3,000–£6,000 to the total.</li>
      <li><strong>Inverter choice:</strong> String inverters are cheaper; optimisers or microinverters cost more but boost efficiency on shaded roofs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Cost Per kW Installed</h2>
    <p>As a benchmark, most UK installers charge somewhere between <strong>£1,400 and £1,800 per kW</strong> installed, with the price per kW generally dropping slightly as system size increases.</p>

    <h2>Payback Period</h2>
    <p>With current electricity prices and export tariffs, most homeowners see a payback period of <strong>6 to 10 years</strong>, with the system continuing to generate savings well beyond that — most panels are rated for 25+ years.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
    <p>Cost is only half the picture — the right system size for your home depends on your roof and energy usage. Getting 2-3 quotes from MCS-certified installers is the best way to see real, comparable pricing for your property.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How much do solar panels cost for a 3-bedroom house in the UK?</h3>
    <p>A typical 3-bedroom home needs a 4kW system, which costs around £6,500–£8,500 fully installed as of 2026, including panels, inverter, scaffolding, and certification.</p>

    <h3>Do I still get 0% VAT on solar panels in 2026?</h3>
    <p>Yes. 0% VAT remains in place on solar panel and battery installations until 31 March 2027, saving the average homeowner £1,000–£2,000 upfront.</p>

    <h3>How much does it cost to add a battery to my solar system?</h3>
    <p>Adding battery storage typically adds £3,000–£6,500 to your total cost, depending on capacity, bringing a full system-plus-battery install to roughly £10,000–£14,000.</p>

    <h3>Are solar panels cheaper in some parts of the UK than others?</h3>
    <p>Yes — installation costs vary significantly by region. The South East tends to be the most expensive (around £9,000+ on average), while the North East is typically the cheapest (around £6,300).</p>

    <h3>How do I calculate solar panel costs for my specific home?</h3>
    <p>Multiply your annual electricity usage in kWh by 0.25 to estimate the minimum system size you need in kW. The average UK home uses around 3,500 kWh/year, which points to a 4kW system for most households.</p>

